{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 25, :comments ["<b>And it came to pass when Rachel had borne Joseph, etc.</b> The reason for the matter, that Jacob did not request 'Send me away, I pray' until after Joseph was born, is that Jacob was uncertain whether twelve tribes would emerge from him or not, as is deduced (BR 68:11): Jacob said, 'If the stones join together one to another, I know that I will establish twelve tribes,' etc. From this we may infer that he was uncertain whether twelve tribes would emerge from him. And when Rachel said in prophecy, 'May YHWH add to me another son' and did not ask for many children, from this we may infer that the spirit of YHWH spoke through her, that twelve tribes would emerge from Jacob and no more. Therefore Jacob said, 'Send me away, I pray,' for then he was confident that Esau would not be able to kill him, since Benjamin had not yet been born, nor was she yet pregnant, for he stayed in Sukkot a long time, approximately two years, and afterward Benjamin was born. And since Jacob was confident that Esau could not prevail against him, therefore he said, 'Send me away, I pray' now, for surely he will do nothing to me, since not all twelve tribes have yet emerged from me." "And our Rabbis, of blessed memory, said (Genesis Rabbah 73:7): When the adversary of Esau was born — that is, Joseph — as it says (Obadiah 1:18), 'And the house of Jacob shall be fire, and the house of Joseph a flame, and the house of Esau stubble,' etc. Even though this verse was spoken of the future, perhaps Jacob knew by the Holy Spirit that Esau's end was to fall by the hand of Joseph, and even though he himself did not see it, his mazal saw it; therefore surely the heart of Esau would fall when he sees Joseph, and therefore he could not prevail against Jacob. And further: Joseph is his adversary, because Esau has no claim against Jacob except that he took the birthright from him, and in the future Joseph will answer him: just as the birthright was taken from Reuben on account of profaning his father's bed and given to Joseph, so by justice it was taken from Esau, who trapped women under their husbands, and given to Jacob — and the receiving of the blessings depends upon the birthright, as is explained above in the portion of Toledot (Genesis 25:31). Another reason: before Rachel gave birth, Jacob was ashamed to come to his father, lest he say that he took a beautiful woman for the sake of desire, for it was for this reason that Rachel said, 'Elohim has taken away my reproach' (Genesis 30:23), for the sensation of not fulfilling the mandate of the species — that is a reproach to us."], :book :genesis, :voice :kli-yakar, :lang :en, :chapter 30, :notes {0 "The citation (בבר״ר סח יא) appears to refer to Bereshit Rabbah 73:7 (not chapter 68); the address is given as found in the source text without normalization.", 1 "The verse citation (בראשית ל לג) appears to be a typographical error for Genesis 30:23, where Rachel says 'אָסַף אֱלֹהִים חֶרְפָּתִי'; translated as found in the text."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T13:31:18.542559879Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}